# Session 05 - Math Display

> This session shows how to write and display formulas in a note, and how to drag a formula from the bottom pane's "MathBox input" onto `math_placement_practice.pdf`.

- `math_display_notes.clro`: this note, with source and rendered results side by side
- `math_placement_practice.pdf`: a practice PDF for placing formulas. Placed formulas are saved in the same-named `.clrop`

Formulas are typeset with MicroTeX and XITS Math. If a formula still shows as source, set the note display mode to "Structured + math". Placing the caret on a formula line shows its source; moving away shows the result again.

## 1. Enclosing a formula

In a note, always enclose a formula in delimiters. An unenclosed `\frac{a}{b}` stays plain text.

```text
Inline: $...$ or \(...\)
Block: $$...$$ or \[...\]
<math>...</math> also works
```

Inline examples: speed is $v = \frac{d}{t}$ and area is \(S = \pi r^2\). A tag also works: <math>E=mc^2</math>.

Block examples:

$$
\int_0^1 x^2\,dx = \frac{1}{3}
$$

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}
\]

## 2. Horizontal position

Inline formulas flow with the text. A tall formula makes its row taller: $\frac{a+b}{\sqrt{c^2+d^2}}$

A block formula is centered in the available width. Inside a `:::` block, it is centered in the remaining indented width.

::: note title="Block formula inside a container"
$$
e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0
$$
:::

A quote (`>`) cannot contain a block formula. Put `$$` at the start of a line.

A formula wider than the available width is neither shrunk nor wrapped; it starts at the left edge and continues to the right. Narrow the note to see the switch.

$$
f(x) = a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + a_3 x^3 + a_4 x^4 + a_5 x^5 + a_6 x^6 + a_7 x^7 + a_8 x^8 + a_9 x^9 + a_{10} x^{10}
$$

## 3. Common notation

| Use | Source | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Scripts | `x_i^2` | $x_i^2$ |
| Fraction | `\frac{a}{b}` | $\frac{a}{b}$ |
| Root | `\sqrt[3]{x}` | $\sqrt[3]{x}$ |
| Greek | `\alpha, \theta, \hbar` | $\alpha, \theta, \hbar$ |
| Sets | `\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{N}` | $\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{N}$ |
| Vector | `\vec{F} = m\vec{a}` | $\vec{F} = m\vec{a}$ |
| Partial | `\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}` | $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}$ |

$$
\nabla \cdot \vec{E} = \frac{\rho}{\varepsilon_0} \qquad
i\hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\psi = \hat{H}\psi
$$

## 4. Matrices, cases and alignment

Only matrices, cases and multi-line alignment need `\begin{name}` ... `\end{name}`. Separate columns with `&` and rows with `\\`.

$$
A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 2 \\
3 & 4
\end{pmatrix}, \quad
\det A = \begin{vmatrix}
1 & 2 \\
3 & 4
\end{vmatrix} = -2
$$

$$
|x| = \begin{cases}
x & (x \ge 0) \\
-x & (x < 0)
\end{cases}
$$

Write `\\[8pt]` to add space between rows.

$$
\begin{aligned}
(a+b)^2 &= a^2 + 2ab + b^2 \\[8pt]
(a-b)^2 &= a^2 - 2ab + b^2
\end{aligned}
$$

Supported environments are `matrix`, `pmatrix`, `bmatrix`, `Bmatrix`, `vmatrix`, `Vmatrix`, `cases`, `aligned` and `align`. Definitions such as `\newcommand` are not available. A formula that cannot be typeset can still be edited and saved as source.

## 5. Switch the bottom pane to formula input and place on a PDF

Separately from note formulas, you can place a formula on a PDF as an annotation.

1. Open `math_placement_practice.pdf` from the list on the left.
2. Select View > Bottom pane (temporary) > MathBox input. The bottom-right pane becomes a formula input with a preview.
3. Type a formula. No `$` delimiters are needed here. Example: `\frac{a+b}{2}`
4. Drag the preview onto a box on the PDF and release. The formula is placed there. Releasing outside a page places nothing.
5. **Add LaTeX MathBox** or Ctrl+Enter places it in the center of the current PDF view.

In the input, a formula that cannot be read as TeX is treated as Markup. Multiple Markup lines are displayed aligned at their starts.

Use Edit in the annotation list to change a placed formula's source, color, or position. The PDF itself is not rewritten; the formula is saved in `math_placement_practice.clrop`.

To return the bottom pane to the note, select View > Bottom pane (temporary) > Extend note text. To change the startup default, use Settings > Bottom pane.
